---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const groups = [
  {
    chapter: "Chapters 1–2",
    title: "Representation and error",
    exercises: [
      {
        id: "B01",
        title: "Make summation order matter",
        task: "Construct a vector containing one very large value and many very small values. Sum it in different orders with ordinary summation and with Kahan summation. Find a case where the order changes the ordinary result enough to notice.",
        record: "The vector, the two orderings, the results, and a short explanation of where the small contributions were lost."
      },
      {
        id: "B02",
        title: "Break the quadratic formula",
        task: "Find coefficients for which the ordinary quadratic formula loses useful digits through cancellation. Compare the two CMNA implementations and explain why a mathematically equivalent form behaves better.",
        record: "The coefficients, both roots from both implementations, and the subtraction that causes the trouble."
      },
      {
        id: "B03",
        title: "Too small to help",
        task: "Use a smooth function with a known derivative. Keep shrinking h in a finite-difference approximation until the error stops improving and begins to grow.",
        record: "A table of h, approximation, and error, plus the range where truncation error gives way to floating-point effects."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    chapter: "Chapter 3",
    title: "Linear algebra",
    exercises: [
      {
        id: "B04",
        title: "Make Jacobi regret meeting your matrix",
        task: "Begin with a diagonally dominant system that converges under Jacobi iteration. Change as few matrix entries as possible until convergence becomes poor or fails.",
        record: "The original and modified matrices, residual histories, and the structural change that mattered."
      },
      {
        id: "B05",
        title: "Reorder before giving up",
        task: "Find a system for which Jacobi behaves badly in the original row order. Reorder the equations and test whether the behavior improves.",
        record: "Both systems, the permutation used, and an explanation of why reordering can change an iterative method without changing the underlying solution."
      },
      {
        id: "B06",
        title: "Ignore structure on purpose",
        task: "Construct a tridiagonal system and solve it once with the dedicated tridiagonal algorithm and once as a generic dense system. Increase the problem size until the structural advantage is hard to ignore.",
        record: "Problem sizes, timings, and a statement of what information the generic solver refused to use."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    chapter: "Chapter 4",
    title: "Interpolation",
    exercises: [
      {
        id: "B07",
        title: "Make the polynomial wave",
        task: "Choose evenly spaced points from a smooth function and increase the polynomial degree until the interpolant develops visibly unreasonable behavior near the ends of the interval.",
        record: "The data points, degree, plot, and the region where fitting every point stopped being reassuring."
      },
      {
        id: "B08",
        title: "Move one point",
        task: "Use the interpolation laboratory with a modest data set. Change one y-value substantially and compare the response of the global polynomial, piecewise linear interpolant, and cubic spline.",
        record: "Before-and-after plots and a description of which methods changed locally and which changed globally."
      },
      {
        id: "B09",
        title: "Extrapolate until it becomes silly",
        task: "Build an interpolant that behaves sensibly inside the data range, then evaluate it progressively farther outside the observed domain.",
        record: "The first point at which you would stop trusting the extrapolation, and why that judgment cannot be recovered from the interpolation fit alone."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    chapter: "Chapter 5",
    title: "Differentiation and integration",
    exercises: [
      {
        id: "B10",
        title: "Give Simpson something awkward",
        task: "Find an integrand for which a coarse Simpson approximation is poor. Increase the panel count, then compare with a smooth integrand where the same coarse rule performs much better.",
        record: "Both functions, panel counts, errors, and the feature of the difficult integrand that required more local resolution."
      },
      {
        id: "B11",
        title: "Spend the same number of evaluations differently",
        task: "Compare Simpson's rule and Gauss-Legendre quadrature on the same smooth function using roughly comparable numbers of function evaluations.",
        record: "Evaluation counts, estimates, errors, and a short explanation of why the node locations matter."
      },
      {
        id: "B12",
        title: "Make Monte Carlo disagree with itself",
        task: "Fix the integrand, interval, and sample count. Run Monte Carlo integration with several seeds and compare the resulting estimates. Then increase the sample count.",
        record: "The seed-to-seed spread at two sample sizes and what changed when m increased."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    chapter: "Chapter 6",
    title: "Root finding and optimization",
    exercises: [
      {
        id: "B13",
        title: "Give bisection an illegal bracket",
        task: "Choose a function with a real root but an interval whose endpoint values have the same sign. Explain why the existence of a root somewhere is not enough for bisection to start.",
        record: "The function, interval, endpoint values, and a repaired bracket."
      },
      {
        id: "B14",
        title: "Make Newton leave",
        task: "Find a function and starting value for which Newton's method moves away from the nearby root, cycles, or encounters a nearly zero derivative.",
        record: "The first several tangent steps and the local feature of the function that caused the behavior."
      },
      {
        id: "B15",
        title: "Make the secant almost horizontal",
        task: "Choose a function and initial value so that two successive function values are nearly equal. Watch what happens when the estimated secant slope becomes very small.",
        record: "The two points defining the problematic secant and the next proposed iterate."
      },
      {
        id: "B16",
        title: "Turn gradient descent into gradient pinball",
        task: "Use a simple convex objective and increase the step size until the iterates overshoot, oscillate, or diverge. Then reduce h until convergence becomes unnecessarily slow.",
        record: "One step size that is too large, one that is useful, one that is too small, and the corresponding paths."
      },
      {
        id: "B17",
        title: "Cool too quickly",
        task: "Run simulated annealing on a multimodal objective with the same starting point and seed but several cooling rates.",
        record: "The best value found under each schedule and evidence of when the search stopped accepting exploratory moves."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    chapter: "Chapter 7",
    title: "Differential equations",
    exercises: [
      {
        id: "B18",
        title: "Let Euler get ahead of itself",
        task: "Choose an initial value problem with a known or high-quality reference solution. Increase h until Euler visibly departs from the reference while RK4 remains useful.",
        record: "The equation, initial condition, step sizes, and final error for both methods."
      },
      {
        id: "B19",
        title: "Make the heat equation explode",
        task: "Hold alpha and dx fixed. Increase dt until the FTCS coefficient crosses the stable range and compare the resulting evolution with a stable run.",
        record: "Both coefficients, the same initial condition, and the first time step where the unstable solution becomes obviously unphysical."
      },
      {
        id: "B20",
        title: "Break the Courant condition",
        task: "Run the wave equation with a Courant number below one, then above one, without changing the initial displacement.",
        record: "The two parameter sets and a description of what the unstable numerical wave does that the physical wave did not request."
      },
      {
        id: "B21",
        title: "A smooth curve is not a certificate",
        task: "Choose any ODE or PDE laboratory and produce a visually smooth numerical solution that is still measurably wrong by changing the discretization.",
        record: "The plot, a quantitative error measure or reference comparison, and an explanation of why appearance alone was misleading."
      }
    ]
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Break It | CMNA Teaching"
  description="Failure-driven numerical-analysis exercises for CMNA."
>
  <header class="page-head teaching-head">
    <div class="shell">
      <p class="eyebrow">Teaching · Assignments</p>
      <h1 class="page-title">Break It</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        A numerical method that only appears in examples chosen to flatter it is not
        being taught very hard. These exercises ask students to find the edge, cross
        it, and explain what failed.
      </p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell two-column">
      <article class="content">
        <p>
          The assignment is not “make the computer produce nonsense.” Computers are
          extraordinarily good at that and need very little encouragement. The useful
          task is to identify an assumption, violate it deliberately, observe the
          numerical consequence, and then connect the consequence back to the method.
        </p>
        <p>
          A good submission should therefore contain the failed case and the repair.
          If Newton runs away, show why the tangent geometry caused it and then choose
          a better starting value. If an explicit PDE scheme becomes unstable, compute
          the parameter that predicts the instability and then bring it back into
          range. The explanation is the assignment. The ugly plot is evidence.
        </p>
      </article>
      <aside class="callout">
        <strong>One rule</strong>
        <p>
          Students should make a prediction before they change the parameter. “I kept
          changing things until the graph looked broken” is experimentation, but it
          is not yet numerical analysis.
        </p>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell break-groups">
      {groups.map((group) => (
        <section class="break-group">
          <div class="break-group-head">
            <span>{group.chapter}</span>
            <h2>{group.title}</h2>
          </div>
          <div class="break-exercise-grid">
            {group.exercises.map((exercise) => (
              <article class="break-exercise">
                <span class="break-id">{exercise.id}</span>
                <h3>{exercise.title}</h3>
                <p>{exercise.task}</p>
                <div class="break-record">
                  <strong>Turn in</strong>
                  <p>{exercise.record}</p>
                </div>
              </article>
            ))}
          </div>
        </section>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
